f 17592186119501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1045 "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dcdc8ecc-c5d8-56ba-b664-0870beb832b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120152 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f59c175-a7f1-58ec-b16f-fd24b9c9dd63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120152 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1061 41645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1061 41646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1061 41660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1061 41667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1061 41674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1061 41675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1064 "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words:\n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n$$L = \\left\\{ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=3x \\right\\}$$\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 3x\\end{aligned}],$ where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above.\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set\n\n$$B=\\left\\{ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=x+1 \\right\\}$$\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1065 6853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is $$\\mathbf{d}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] - [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] .$$ This means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is $$\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1065 6859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1067 "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 4x-2y\\end{aligned}], \\qquad x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}.$$\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above.\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1065 6860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1067 "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272628 #edb/ref 1080 "IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272628 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272628 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c083987-1ed3-5e4c-bd55-30281759ca50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272682 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272682 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272682 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21412424-3f98-56c9-a40e-7ec7f1e6afe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272841 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272841 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272841 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32e12cd0-8ad2-5342-a83f-ccbf2317d4a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273013 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273013 #edb/